{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CgS7BhZTY4MdmgAmsGmPP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017vC5KKurbZLhpxRzK7gtHE","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/h-3awxz0/ANSWER.md","content":"# HR targets clamped to the watch's zones\n\n## What was wrong\n\n`gen.py` wrote every HR corridor as\n\n```json\n{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"value\": 88, \"end\": 93}\n```\n\nIntervals.icu's workout format uses `start` for the lower bound of a range and\n`value` for a single target. With `value` as the lower bound and `end` as the\nupper bound, the range is not read as a corridor. The export then falls back to\na single target and Garmin snaps it to the nearest of its own zone bands. That\nmatches the readback: the 151–160 corridor came out as the Z4 band 151–162, and\nthe other corridors also landed on zone edges.\n\nThe readback also rules out two other causes. Using `units: \"bpm\"` with\nabsolute values made the watch show 214–249 bpm, so the units were not the\nproblem. `start_bpm`/`end_bpm` produced a constant 101, which means the export\ndid not handle those keys as ranges either.\n\n## What changed\n\n`gen.py`, `hr_target()`: `\"value\"` → `\"start\"`. The `end` key and the `%lthr`\nunits are unchanged. Output for `plans/tempo-run.json` now contains, for example:\n\n```json\n{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\": 88, \"end\": 93}\n```\n\nEvery step with an `hr_pct` range is affected, including the repeated Tempo and\nFloat steps.\n\n## What was not verified\n\nI could not check this against a live push or the watch. Pushing to Intervals.icu\nis outside this task, and the web search did not turn up the official\n`workout_doc` schema. The `start`/`end` convention is inferred from the power\nexample in the forum thread and from the readback. Confirm it by:\n\n1. Running `./push.sh event.json` with the regenerated file, then\n2. Checking that `GET /api/v1/athlete/{id}/events/{eventId}` or the `download`\n   endpoint shows `start`/`end` on the HR targets, and that the watch displays\n   151–160 for Tempo, 129–141 for Float, 117–129 for Warm-up, and 112–124 for\n   Cool-down after re-sync.\n\nIf the watch still shows zone bands after that, the next thing to check is the\nathlete's zone configuration in Intervals.icu, and whether the export uses zone\ntargets for HR rather than custom ranges.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1810,"cache_read_input_tokens":26378,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1810},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b6543445-6ad1-4b9e-8dc7-218bf60ae8fb","uuid":"fcd431d6-e6b3-4695-bdbb-c6d03164809d","timestamp":"2026-10-27T04:25:55.536Z","request_id":"req_011CgS7BhLouCJEZJcJkUMZF","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_017vC5KKurbZLhpxRzK7gtHE":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/h-3awxz0/ANSWER.md","content":"# HR targets clamped to the watch's zones\n\n## What was wrong\n\n`gen.py` wrote every HR corridor as\n\n```json\n{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"value\": 88, \"end\": 93}\n```\n\nIntervals.icu's workout format uses `start` for the lower bound of a range and\n`value` for a single target. With `value` as the lower bound and `end` as the\nupper bound, the range is not read as a corridor. The export then falls back to\na single target and Garmin snaps it to the nearest of its own zone bands. That\nmatches the readback: the 151–160 corridor came out as the Z4 band 151–162, and\nthe other corridors also landed on zone edges.\n\nThe readback also rules out two other causes. Using `units: \"bpm\"` with\nabsolute values made the watch show 214–249 bpm, so the units were not the\nproblem. `start_bpm`/`end_bpm` produced a constant 101, which means the export\ndid not handle those keys as ranges either.\n\n## What changed\n\n`gen.py`, `hr_target()`: `\"value\"` → `\"start\"`. The `end` key and the `%lthr`\nunits are unchanged. Output for `plans/tempo-run.json` now contains, for example:\n\n```json\n{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\": 88, \"end\": 93}\n```\n\nEvery step with an `hr_pct` range is affected, including the repeated Tempo and\nFloat steps.\n\n## What was not verified\n\nI could not check this against a live push or the watch. Pushing to Intervals.icu\nis outside this task, and the web search did not turn up the official\n`workout_doc` schema. The `start`/`end` convention is inferred from the power\nexample in the forum thread and from the readback. Confirm it by:\n\n1. Running `./push.sh event.json` with the regenerated file, then\n2. Checking that `GET /api/v1/athlete/{id}/events/{eventId}` or the `download`\n   endpoint shows `start`/`end` on the HR targets, and that the watch displays\n   151–160 for Tempo, 129–141 for Float, 117–129 for Warm-up, and 112–124 for\n   Cool-down after re-sync.\n\nIf the watch still shows zone bands after that, the next thing to check is the\nathlete's zone configuration in Intervals.icu, and whether the export uses zone\ntargets for HR rather than custom ranges.\n"}}}
